-- title:
--   Hecke–Galois datum over the localised Hecke algebra, with local conditions
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be an odd prime, $N\ge 1$, and let $S$ be a finite set of natural numbers all of whose members are prime, with $p\in S$, with every prime divisor of $N$ in $S$, and with $p^2\nmid N$. Let $\mathcal O$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain of characteristic zero, complete for its maximal-ideal adic topology, with finite residue field, and with $p$ in its maximal ideal. Assume the weight-two cusp forms for $\Gamma_0(N)$ admit an integral structure, that is, the forms all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are integers span the whole space over $\mathbb C$. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of weight-two cusp forms of level $N$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$) and $U_q$ ($q$ prime, $q\mid N$, $q\notin S$), to the residue field $k$ of $\mathcal O$, and let $\bar\rho$ be a two-dimensional representation of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ over $k$ factoring through a finite extension of $\mathbb Q$, absolutely irreducible (irreducible after base change to $\overline k$), and satisfying, for every prime $\ell\nmid N$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\ell$ a non-unit in $P$ and every $\sigma$ that is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $P$, the identity $\operatorname{charpoly}(\bar\rho(\sigma))=X^2-\theta(T_\ell)X+\ell$; if $p\mid N$ assume moreover that $\bar\rho$, viewed as an adic representation over $k$, is ordinary at $p$. Write $\mathbb T=$ [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) for the localisation of the $\mathcal O$-base change of the Hecke lattice at the prime cut out by $\theta$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a [`CuspForm.HeckeGaloisRepDatum`](def/CuspForm_HeckeGaloisRepDatum.html#L8) $H$ for $N$, $S$, $\mathcal O$, $\theta$ over $\mathbb T$ — thus a ring homomorphism $\pi$ from the Hecke algebra to $\mathbb T$ whose reduction is $\theta$, with $\mathcal O[\operatorname{im}\pi]=\mathbb T$, with every character into $\mathcal O$ lifting $\theta$ factoring through $\pi$, with residue map surjective on $\mathcal O$, and a free rank-two adically continuous representation $\rho$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ over $\mathbb T$ with $\operatorname{charpoly}(\rho(\mathrm{Frob}_\ell))=X^2-\pi(T_\ell)X+\ell$ for $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$, and absolutely irreducible residual representation — such that: $\pi$ is the canonical map [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151); $\det\rho$ is cyclotomic at $p$, in the sense that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $\mathbb T$ and $\det\rho(\sigma)\equiv a \pmod{p^n}$ whenever $\sigma$ raises all $p^n$-th roots of unity to the $a$-th power; $\rho$ is unramified at every prime $q\nmid N$ with $q\ne p$; inertia at every prime $q\ne p$ with $q\mid N$, $q^2\nmid N$ acts with characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$; $\rho$ is ordinary at $p$ if $p\mid N$ and flat at $p$ if $p\nmid N$; the residual representation of $\rho$ is isomorphic to the base change of $\bar\rho$ along the induced map of residue fields; and for every prime $q\nmid N$ with $q\ne p$ there is $t\in\mathbb T$ with $\operatorname{charpoly}(\rho(\sigma))=X^2-tX+q$ for every Frobenius $\sigma$ at $q$.
--
--   This is the Eichler–Shimura–Deligne construction of the two-dimensional Galois representation carried by the local factor of the weight-two Hecke algebra at a residual eigensystem $\theta$, packaged with the local behaviour (Eichler–Shimura relations away from the level, unipotent inertia at primes exactly dividing the level, ordinary at $p$ dividing the level, finite flat at $p$ prime to the level) and with the identification of its reduction with $\bar\rho$. It is the input to the study of the localised Hecke algebra as a deformation ring, and is used in the construction of patching data and in the proof that the map from the deformation ring to the Hecke algebra is bijective.
